window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"Statistics starts with a question","u":"module-1.html#intro","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Statistics is about gathering, organizing, analyzing, and interpreting data. Before any of that, answer three questions: what group or scientific question are we focusing on, which members of that group (or which questions about it) do we want to explore, and how will we gather and organize the data? Identify the population, the sample, and the variable in a study. Classify a variable as categorical (nominal or ordinal) or quantitative (discrete or continuous). Recognize simple random, systematic, stratified, and cluster sampling. Describe a distribution by its shape, center, spread, and outliers. Compute the mean, median, quartiles, IQR, sample variance, and sample standard deviation by hand, and identify outliers. Predict what shifting or rescaling data does to x̄ and s, and choose summaries that fit the data. Example scientific question Three-toed sloths Is it true that three-toed sloths move at an average speed of 4 meters per minute? We cannot time every sloth, so we time a sample of sloths and use their speeds to learn about all of them. This question returns in Chapters 7 and 8."},{"t":"Population, sample, and variables","u":"module-1.html#population","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The population is the complete collection of all measurements or data being considered. It is not necessarily people, it often has millions or billions (even infinitely many) members, and it is generally never fully observed. A sample is a subset of the population, usually a small percentage of it. A variable is any characteristic of an individual that is of interest; it changes from subject to subject. There are two types. Categorical (qualitative): each observation belongs to one of several groups. Nominal groups have no order (blood type A, B, AB, O; eye color). Ordinal groups have a natural order (socioeconomic status low, medium, high; education level). Quantitative: observations are numbers for which arithmetic makes sense. Discrete variables take only certain fixed values with nothing in between (number of computers owned, number of pets, age in whole years). Continuous variables can take infinitely many values between any two points (height, weight, income). Notation. Lowercase letters name variables: x might be the weight of newborn calves, y the number of defective items in a shipment, z a patient's blood type. A sample of size n from x is written x1, x2, , xn . Worked example Identify the population, sample, and variable (a) A survey of 1900 students from college campuses across the US found an average student loan debt of $42,315. (b) A 2018 Brookings poll wanted the percentage of adult US internet users who believe robots will perform most human activities in 30 years. (c) A 2019 ESPN poll found that 68% of adult NBA fans believe the Mavericks have the best future among non-playoff teams. (a) Population: all US college students. Sample: the 1900 students surveyed. Variable: student loan debt in dollars. Type: quantitative and continuous. (Money is often treated as continuous because it takes so many values.) (b) Population: all adult internet users in the US. Sample: the adults polled by Brookings. Variable: whether the person believes robots will perform most human activities in 30 years (yes or no). Type: categorical and nominal. (c) Population: all adult NBA fans. Sample: the fans in the ESPN poll. Variable: whether the fan believes the Mavericks have the best future among non-playoff teams (yes or no). Type: categorical and nominal. The 68% summarizes the sample, not the whole population. Watch out. Numbers are not always quantitative. A zip code or a jersey number is a label, so it is categorical. A percentage reported for a yes/no question (like the 68%) summarizes a categorical variable."},{"t":"Sampling methods","u":"module-1.html#sampling","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"How the sample is chosen decides whether it can stand in for the population. Four methods appear in this course. Simple random sampling (SRS): every possible sample of a given size has an equal chance of being selected. Systematic sampling: pick a random starting point between 1 and k, then include every kth subject after it. Stratified sampling: divide the population into homogeneous groups called strata (members are similar with respect to the variable of interest), then take an SRS within each stratum. Cluster sampling: divide the population into heterogeneous groups called clusters (each cluster looks like the population as a whole), randomly select clusters, and include everyone in the chosen clusters. Worked example An SRS of size 2 from {A, B, C, D} List every possible sample of size 2: {A,B}, {A,C}, {A,D}, {B,C}, {B,D}, {C,D}. There are 6. In an SRS each of the 6 samples is equally likely, so each has probability 1/6. Each individual appears in 3 of the 6 samples, so each individual is chosen with probability 3/6 = 1/2. Equal chances for individuals alone is not enough for an SRS; every sample must be equally likely. Worked example Systematic sampling A quality engineer wants a sample from 500 items coming off a line and chooses k = 25. Choose a random start between 1 and 25, say 7. Take every 25th item after it: 7, 32, 57, 82, … The jth item chosen is in position 7 + (j − 1)(25). The sample has 500/25 = 20 items. The 20th is in position 7 + 19(25) = 482. Stratified or cluster? In stratified sampling you sample within every group, and the groups are internally similar. In cluster sampling you sample whole groups , and each group is a small copy of the population."},{"t":"Distributions: tables and graphs","u":"module-1.html#distribution","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The distribution of a variable describes how often (frequency) or what fraction of the time (relative frequency) the variable takes each value. Some outcomes are more likely than others: adult heights fall between 5 and 6 feet far more often than between 3 and 4 feet. The population distribution is usually unknown, so we study the distribution of a sample and look for its shape, center, spread, and outliers . relative frequency= frequencyn Here n is the total number of observations. Frequency tables give the count (and relative frequency, as a proportion or percentage) for each value or range of values. Pie charts and bar charts summarize categorical variables. Histograms are bar graphs of binned quantitative data; the height of each bar is the frequency (or relative frequency) of values in that bin. Boxplots graph the five-number summary (Section 6). Reading a histogram. Where are the values concentrated? How spread out are they? Is the shape symmetric or skewed? Are there gaps or clusters? Are there potential outliers? A histogram of hits per nine-inning game rises smoothly to a single peak and then declines, extending a bit further to the right than to the left: a slight positive (right) skew . Skew is named for the longer tail. Worked example Salary survey: are both majors equally represented? A university surveyed 2,232 recent alumni who majored in engineering or education. The variables are gender (categorical, nominal), major (categorical, nominal), and salary in dollars (quantitative, continuous). There were 1,076 education majors and 1,156 engineering majors. Total: 1,076 + 1,156 = 2,232. Relative frequency of education: 1,076/2,232 = 0.4821, about 48.2%. Relative frequency of engineering: 1,156/2,232 = 0.5179, about 51.8%. The relative frequencies add to 1. The two majors are close to equally represented, with slightly more engineers. R counts <- c(Education = 1076, Engineering = 1156); counts / sum(counts) Python counts = {\"Education\": 1076, \"Engineering\": 1156}; n = sum(counts.values()); {k: v / n for k, v in counts.items()}"},{"t":"Measures of location: mean, median, mode","u":"module-1.html#location","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The sample mean uses every observation. The sample median x is the middle observation once the values are sorted. The sample mode is the value (or values) that occurs most often. x= x1+x2+ +xnn= 1n i=1nxi n odd: x = the ( n+12 )th ordered value n even: x = average of the ( n2 )th and ( n2+1 )th values Worked example Sloth speeds Seven sloths were timed (meters per minute): 4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7. Sum: 4.3 + 2.8 + 4.0 + 5.1 + 3.4 + 4.6 + 3.7 = 27.9. Mean: x̄ = 27.9/7 = 3.9857 m/min . Sort: 2.8, 3.4, 3.7, 4.0, 4.3, 4.6, 5.1. n = 7 is odd, so the median is the (7 + 1)/2 = 4th ordered value: x̃ = 4.0 m/min . No value repeats, so there is no single mode. Now suppose the 5.1 had been recorded as 10.8. The sum becomes 33.6 and x̄ = 4.8 m/min , but the 4th ordered value is still 4.0. The median is resistant to the extreme value; the mean is pulled toward it. R x <- c(4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7); mean(x); median(x) Python import numpy as np; x = np.array([4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7]); x.mean(), np.median(x) Common mistakes. Forgetting to sort before finding the median. With an even n, picking one of the two middle values instead of averaging them. Reporting the position (n + 1)/2 instead of the value in that position."},{"t":"Quartiles, percentiles, and boxplots","u":"module-1.html#quartiles","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The pth percentile is a value with about p × 100% of the data below it and about (1 − p) × 100% above it. The first quartile Q1 is the 25th percentile, the second quartile Q2 is the 50th percentile (the median), and the third quartile Q3 is the 75th percentile. The five-number summary is min, Q1 , Q2 , Q3 , max. Our convention for quartiles. Sort the data and find the median. Split the data into a lower half and an upper half, leaving the median out of both halves when n is odd . Q1 is the median of the lower half and Q3 is the median of the upper half. Five-number summary: ,\\;Q1,\\; x,\\;Q3,\\; IQR=Q3-Q1 Worked example Five-number summary by hand Ten observations: 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197. The data are already sorted. n = 10 is even, so the median is (12 + 18)/2 = 15. Lower half: 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, so Q1 = 7 . Upper half: 18, 23, 25, 98, 197, so Q3 = 25 . Five-number summary: (1, 7, 15, 25, 197). IQR = 25 − 7 = 18. R x <- c(1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197); median(x[1:5]); median(x[6:10]) R's quantile(x) and summary(x) interpolate and may differ from our convention. Python import numpy as np; x = np.sort([1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197]); np.median(x[:5]), np.median(x[5:]) np.percentile interpolates and may differ from our convention. Boxplots and histograms show the same features: shape, center, spread, and outliers. The box runs from Q1 to Q3 with a line at the median; whiskers reach to the most extreme values that are not outliers, and outliers are plotted separately. Skewness in a histogram appears in the boxplot as a longer whisker (longer tail) on the side of the skew . Watch out. With an odd n, do not include the median in either half. And do not confuse the IQR ( Q3 - Q1 ) with the range (max − min)."},{"t":"Measures of variability","u":"module-1.html#variability","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The deviations from the mean are x1- x,\\;x2- x, ,xn- x . They always add to zero, so their plain average cannot measure spread; we square them first. i=1n(xi- x)=0 The interquartile range , IQR=Q3-Q1 , is the range of the middle 50% of the data. The textbook calls it the fourth spread , fs . The sample variance averages the squared deviations with the divisor n − 1, and the sample standard deviation is its square root, back in the original units. s2= 1n-1 i=1n(xi- x)2= Sxxn-1 Sxx= i=1nxi2- 1n ( i=1nxi )2 s= s2 The divisor n − 1 accounts for the one degree of freedom lost by estimating the center with x̄. Both s2 and s are always nonnegative, and s = 0 only when every observation is identical. Worked example s two ways for 2, 4, 5, 9, 10 Mean: x̄ = (2 + 4 + 5 + 9 + 10)/5 = 30/5 = 6. Deviations: −4, −2, −1, 3, 4. They add to 0. Squared deviations: 16, 4, 1, 9, 16. Sum: Sxx=46 . Shortcut check: xi2=4+16+25+81+100=226 , and Sxx=226- 3025=226-180=46 . Same answer. Variance: s2=46/(5-1)=11.5 . Standard deviation: s= 11.5=3.3912 . R x <- c(2, 4, 5, 9, 10); var(x); sd(x) (R divides by n − 1) Python import numpy as np; x = np.array([2, 4, 5, 9, 10]); x.var(ddof=1), x.std(ddof=1) ( ddof=1 gives n − 1; the default divides by n) Red-pencil reminder. Divide by n − 1, not n. Do not report the variance when the standard deviation is asked for. In the shortcut, square the sum after adding: ( xi)2 is not xi2 ."},{"t":"Outliers","u":"module-1.html#outliers","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Outliers are values far outside the overall pattern of a distribution. Always try to explain an outlier; it may be a recording error, or it may be the most interesting point in the data. A common rule of thumb flags an observation as an outlier when it lies beyond 1.5 IQR from the box. x<Q1-1.5 IQR or x>Q3+1.5 IQR Boxplots often separate mild outliers (beyond 1.5 IQR) from extreme outliers (beyond 3 IQR), as in the nitrogen-load boxplot from class. Worked example Salary fences by major Summary statistics for salary: Education Q1=34,478.5 , Q3=36,519.5 ; Engineering Q1=54,092 , Q3=57,799 . Education: IQR = 36,519.5 − 34,478.5 = 2,041. Fences: 34,478.5 − 1.5(2,041) = 31,417 and 36,519.5 + 1.5(2,041) = 39,581. Engineering: IQR = 57,799 − 54,092 = 3,707. Fences: 54,092 − 1.5(3,707) = 48,531.5 and 57,799 + 1.5(3,707) = 63,359.5. An education salary of $40,000 would be an outlier for education majors (above 39,581), while the same salary is far below every engineering fence: an outlier there in the other direction. \"Outlier\" always means \"unusual relative to this distribution.\" About outliers. Never delete an outlier just because it is extreme. Fix it if it is a recording error; otherwise keep it. If you report results without it, say so and say why."},{"t":"Shifting and scaling data","u":"module-1.html#transform","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Changing units changes the summaries in a predictable way. Adding a constant moves every value, and the center, by that constant but leaves the spread alone. Multiplying by a positive constant multiplies both the center and the spread. yj=xj+C\\; \\; y= x+C,\\;\\;sy=sx yj=Cxj\\;(C>0)\\; \\; y=C x,\\;\\;sy=Csx Proof sketch Why the proposition holds Shift: y= 1n (xj+C)= x+C . Each deviation is unchanged: yj- y=(xj+C)-( x+C)=xj- x , so sy=sx . Scale: y= 1n Cxj=C x . Each deviation is multiplied by C: yj- y=C(xj- x) , so sy2=C2sx2 and sy=Csx because C > 0. Worked example Sloth speeds in other units For the seven sloths, x̄ = 3.9857 m/min and s = 0.7690 m/min . In centimeters per minute every speed is multiplied by 100: ȳ = 398.57 cm/min and sy = 76.90 cm/min . If the stopwatch added 0.2 m/min to every reading, the mean becomes 4.1857 m/min and s stays 0.7690 m/min . Watch out. A shift never changes s (or the IQR). A scale factor multiplies s by C, but multiplies the variance by C2 ."},{"t":"Choosing measures of location and variability","u":"module-1.html#choosing","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"For symmetric data without outliers , the sample mean x̄ and the sample standard deviation s are good summaries. For skewed data or data with outliers , the median x̃ and the IQR are usually preferred, because they are resistant. In skewed data the mean is pulled toward the longer tail. Worked example Do salaries differ across majors? Summary statistics: Education n = 1,076, mean $35,183.06, median $35,640.50; Engineering n = 1,156, mean $56,004.89, median $55,660. Center: the engineering median is about $20,000 higher than the education median. Salaries clearly differ by major. Spread: the engineering IQR (3,707) is larger than the education IQR (2,041). Shape: for education the mean is below the median, which suggests a slight left skew; for engineering the mean is above the median, which suggests a slight right skew. Because the shapes are not perfectly symmetric, comparing medians and IQRs is the safer choice. Describe a distribution in context. Name the shape, give a center and a spread that suit the shape, mention outliers, and include units."},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-1.html#summary","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Identify the population, sample, and variable, and classify the variable. Choose a display that fits the variable: bar or pie chart for categorical data, histogram or boxplot for quantitative data. Describe shape, center, spread, and outliers. x= 1n xi s2= Sxxn-1 IQR=Q3-Q1 outlier: beyond Q1-1.5\\,IQR or Q3+1.5\\,IQR R and Python for engineers. Work by hand first. To check: R uses mean , median , var , sd , IQR , boxplot , hist ; Python (NumPy) uses np.mean , np.median , np.var(x, ddof=1) , np.std(x, ddof=1) , and Matplotlib's plt.hist and plt.boxplot . Software quartiles (and so the IQR) can differ slightly from our hand convention."},{"t":"Experiments, sample spaces, and events","u":"module-2.html#definitions","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Probability is the limiting value of the proportion of times an outcome occurs as the number of repeated trials goes to infinity. It is the foundation of statistical inference, because inference is based on random samples. Write the sample space of an experiment and describe events as sets. Use unions, intersections, complements, and Venn diagrams. Apply the axioms, the complement rule, and the addition rule. Find probabilities with equally likely outcomes and from two-way tables. Compute conditional probabilities, check independence, and use the multiplication rule. Use the law of total probability and Bayes’ theorem. An experiment is any activity or process whose outcome is subject to uncertainty. The sample space S is the collection of all possible outcomes; for a die roll, S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. An event (A, B, C, …) is any subset of the sample space. A simple event has exactly one outcome (rolling a 4: {4}); a compound event has more than one (rolling an even number: {2, 4, 6}). A probability is a number between 0 and 1, inclusive."},{"t":"Set operations and Venn diagrams","u":"module-2.html#sets","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"The union A B (“A or B”) contains every outcome in A, in B, or in both. The intersection A B (“A and B”) contains the outcomes in both. The complement A' contains every outcome in S that is not in A. The null set ∅ has no outcomes. Events A and B are mutually exclusive (disjoint) if A B= . S A B A∩B The overlap is A ∩ B. Everything inside either circle is A ∪ B. Everything in S outside A is A′. Worked example A die roll Roll one die. Let A = {2, 4, 6} (even) and B = {4, 5, 6} (more than 3). A B=\\2,4,5,6\\ and A B=\\4,6\\ . A'=\\1,3,5\\ and B'=\\1,2,3\\ . A and B are not mutually exclusive, because they share 4 and 6. A and A' are always mutually exclusive."},{"t":"Axioms and properties of probability","u":"module-2.html#axioms","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Probability theory assigns each event A a number P(A) that measures the chance that A occurs; it can be viewed as the long-run proportion of times A occurs in many repeated trials. Toss a fair coin 100 times and the percentage of heads may be far from 50%; toss it 100,000 times and the percentage settles close to 50%. Axioms. (1) P(A) 0 for any event A. (2) P(S)=1 . (3) For disjoint events A1,A2,A3, , P(A1 A2 )= i=1 P(Ai) . From the axioms: P( )=0 and 0 P(A) 1 for any A. P(A')=1-P(A) P(A B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A B) The addition rule rearranges to P(A B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A B) . For three events, the inclusion–exclusion rule adds the singles, subtracts the pairs, and adds back the triple. P(A B C)=P(A)+P(B)+P(C) -P(A B)-P(B C)-P(A C) +P(A B C) Worked example Addition rule In a plant, 30% of parts have a surface flaw (A), 20% have a dimension flaw (B), and 8% have both. P(A B)=0.30+0.20-0.08=0.42 : 42% have at least one flaw. P(no flaw)=1-0.42=0.58 . P(A B')=P(A)-P(A B)=0.30-0.08=0.22 : surface flaw only. Watch out. Adding P(A) + P(B) counts the overlap twice. Subtract P(A B) unless the events are mutually exclusive."},{"t":"Equally likely outcomes","u":"module-2.html#equally-likely","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"If S has N outcomes that are all equally likely, each outcome has probability 1/N, and an event A with N(A) outcomes has probability N(A)/N. P(outcome)= 1N P(A)= N(A)N Worked example Two coin tosses Toss a fair coin twice. Let A be the event that at least one toss is heads. S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}: N = 4 equally likely outcomes. A = {HH, HT, TH}, so P(A)=3/4=0.75 . No heads is the complement: P(A')=P(\\TT\\)=1/4=0.25=1-0.75 . Worked example A statistics class A class has 30 students: 15 are physically present but mentally absent and 15 are physically and mentally absent. One student is selected at random. N = 30 equally likely students; N(A) = 15 are physically present but mentally absent. P(A)=15/30=0.5 ."},{"t":"Conditional probability","u":"module-2.html#conditional","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"If B has occurred, the probability that A occurs is the conditional probability of A given B . Conditioning shrinks the sample space to B. P(A B)= P(A B)P(B), P(B)>0 Worked example Gender vs opinion A survey asked whether the emergence of infectious diseases in other countries is a serious threat to the United States. Yes No Undecided Total Male 144 22 88 254 Female 159 24 48 231 Total 303 46 136 485 A randomly chosen male answered No: P(No | Male) = 22/254 = 0.0866. A randomly chosen Yes respondent is female: P(Female | Yes) = 159/303 = 0.5248. Females: P(No | Female) = 24/231 = 0.1039, which is more than 0.0866, so females are slightly more likely than males to answer No. Undecided overall: 136/485 = 0.2804, about 28.0%. Watch out. P(A | B) and P(B | A) are different. The event after the bar is the group you restrict to, so its total goes in the denominator."},{"t":"The multiplication rule and independence","u":"module-2.html#independence","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Rearranging the definition of conditional probability gives the multiplication rule . Events A and B are independent when knowing that B occurred does not change the probability of A. P(A B)=P(A B)\\,P(B) Independent P(A B)=P(A) P(A B)=P(A)P(B) It can also be shown that P(A B)+P(A' B)=1 and P(A)=P(A B)P(B)+P(A B')P(B') . Events A1, ,An are mutually independent if the probability of the intersection of every subset of them equals the product of their probabilities. Worked example Down syndrome blood test Status Positive Negative Total D (Down) 48 6 54 D′ (Unaffected) 1307 3921 5228 Total 1355 3927 5282 P(Positive) = 1355/5282 = 0.2565. P(Positive | D) = 48/54 = 0.8889. 0.8889 ≠ 0.2565, so the test result and Down syndrome status are not independent : a positive result is far more likely when the condition is present. Equivalent check: P(D ∩ Positive) = 48/5282 = 0.0091, but P(D)P(Positive) = (54/5282)(0.2565) = 0.0026. Worked example Shoulder surgery An operation succeeds on the first attempt with probability 0.80. If it fails, a second operation succeeds with probability 0.98. P(first fails) = 1 − 0.80 = 0.20 and P(second fails | first fails) = 1 − 0.98 = 0.02. Multiplication rule: P(both fail) = (0.20)(0.02) = 0.004. Independent is not the same as mutually exclusive. Mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities are never independent: if one happens, the other cannot."},{"t":"Total probability and Bayes’ theorem","u":"module-2.html#bayes","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Let A1, ,Ak be a partition of S: mutually exclusive and exhaustive events. The law of total probability finds P(B) by adding the branch products of a tree. Bayes’ theorem reverses the condition: from the prior probabilities P(Ai) and the likelihoods P(B Ai) it gives the posterior probability P(Aj B) . P(B)= i=1kP(B Ai)\\,P(Ai) P(Aj B)= P(B Aj)\\,P(Aj) i=1kP(B Ai)\\,P(Ai) Worked example A pregnancy test 55% of women who use a pregnancy test are pregnant. The test reads positive for 99% of pregnant women and negative for 99.2% of women who are not pregnant. Partition: pregnant (0.55) and not pregnant (0.45). P(+ | pregnant) = 0.99 and P(+ | not pregnant) = 1 − 0.992 = 0.008. Total probability: P(+) = 0.99(0.55) + 0.008(0.45) = 0.5445 + 0.0036 = 0.5481. Bayes: P(pregnant | +) = 0.5445/0.5481 = 0.9934. Worked example Three machines Machines M1, M2, M3 make 40%, 35%, and 25% of the parts; 2%, 3%, and 5% of their parts are defective (D). P(D) = 0.02(0.40) + 0.03(0.35) + 0.05(0.25) = 0.0080 + 0.0105 + 0.0125 = 0.0310. P(M3 | D) = 0.0125/0.0310 = 0.4032. M3 makes only 25% of the parts but about 40% of the defectives. R prior <- c(0.40, 0.35, 0.25); pD <- c(0.02, 0.03, 0.05); joint <- prior * pD; sum(joint); joint / sum(joint) Python prior = [0.40, 0.35, 0.25]; pD = [0.02, 0.03, 0.05]; joint = [a*b for a, b in zip(prior, pD)]; sum(joint), [j/sum(joint) for j in joint] Natural frequencies. Imagine 10,000 parts: 4,000 from M1 (80 defective), 3,500 from M2 (105 defective), 2,500 from M3 (125 defective). Of the 310 defectives, 125 came from M3: 125/310 = 0.4032."},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-2.html#summary","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Describe the sample space and events with sets. Use the complement and addition rules, count equally likely outcomes, and restrict to the given group for a conditional probability. Multiply along a tree, add the branches for a total probability, and divide by that total to reverse the condition with Bayes’ theorem. P(A')=1-P(A) P(A B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A B) P(A B)= P(A B)P(B) P(A B)=P(A B)P(B) R and Python for engineers. Probability calculations here are arithmetic. To simulate and check a long-run proportion: R mean(sample(c(\"H\",\"T\"), 1e5, replace = TRUE) == \"H\") ; Python import numpy as np; (np.random.default_rng().integers(0, 2, 100000) == 1).mean() ."},{"t":"Random variables","u":"module-3.html#random-variables","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"In Chapter 1 we recorded “variables.” From now on we say random variable , because the value we observe comes from a random experiment or a random sampling process. For a sample space S, a random variable (RV) is a rule that assigns a real number to each outcome in S: a function whose domain is S and whose range is the real numbers. Tell discrete from continuous random variables. Use a PMF, a probability table, and a CDF, including P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a−). Compute E(X), E[h(X)], Var(X), and SD by hand, and use the rules for aX + b. Recognize the binomial, hypergeometric, negative binomial, and Poisson settings and compute their probabilities, means, and variances. Approximate a binomial by a Poisson when n > 50 and np < 5. Notation. Uppercase letters X, Y, Z name random variables, X:S R ; lowercase letters are particular values: X(s)=x . For one coin flip, S = {H, T} and X(H) = 1, X(T) = 0, so X counts heads. Other examples: X = student loan debt of a randomly chosen student; Y = 1 for a “yes” answer and 0 for “no.” In a random sample, Y1, ,Yn are independent copies of Y."},{"t":"Discrete and continuous random variables","u":"module-3.html#types","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A discrete random variable has possible values that form a finite set or a countably infinite list (a first value, a second value, and so on): the number of defective items in a sample, X ∈ {0, 1, …, 10}; the number of trials until the first success, X ∈ {1, 2, 3, …}. A continuous random variable has possible values that fill an entire interval: the time to failure of a machine, X ∈ (0, ∞); the measurement error of a sensor, X ∈ (−∞, ∞). Discrete Continuous Number rolled on a die Time to complete an exam Number of accidents in a day Amount of rainfall in Dallas Number of misprints per page Monthly spending of a student"},{"t":"Probability distributions and the PMF","u":"module-3.html#pmf","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"The probability distribution of X describes how the total probability of 1 is allocated among the possible values of X. For a discrete RV it can be given by a probability mass function, a probability table, or a probability histogram. The probability mass function (PMF) is p(x)=P(X=x) , read “the probability that X takes the value x.” p(x) 0 all xp(x)=1 Worked example Coin flips Flip a fair coin twice (independent flips) and let X count the heads. x 0 1 2 P(X = x) 1/4 1/2 1/4 The four outcomes HH, HT, TH, TT are equally likely; one has 0 heads, two have 1, one has 2. P(X > 0) = 1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4, or 1 − P(X = 0) = 3/4. P(X ≤ 2) = 1: every possible value is at most 2. A Bernoulli random variable takes only the values 0 and 1, with P(X=1)=p and P(X=0)=1-p . One fair coin flip (1 = heads) is Bernoulli with p = 0.5."},{"t":"The cumulative distribution function","u":"module-3.html#cdf","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"The cumulative distribution function (CDF) of a discrete RV gives the probability that X is at most x. It is a step function that jumps by p(x) at each possible value. F(x)=P(X x)= y xp(y) P(a X b)=F(b)-F(a-) Here F(a-) is the left-hand limit at a; for integer values it is F(a − 1). Worked example CDF for two coin flips F(0) = P(X = 0) = 1/4. F(1) = 1/4 + 1/2 = 3/4. F(2) = 1/4 + 1/2 + 1/4 = 1. So F(x) = 0 for x < 0; 1/4 for 0 ≤ x < 1; 3/4 for 1 ≤ x < 2; and 1 for x ≥ 2. P(1 ≤ X ≤ 2) = F(2) − F(0) = 1 − 1/4 = 3/4. Watch out. For a discrete RV, P(X < b) and P(X ≤ b) differ by P(X = b). Translate the words first: “fewer than 11” is X ≤ 10."},{"t":"Expected value","u":"module-3.html#expected","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"The expected value (expectation, mean) of X is the probability-weighted average of its values. Think of it as the long-run average of many independent observations of X. E(X)= X= x Dx\\,p(x) Worked example Two coin flips E(X) = 0(1/4) + 1(1/2) + 2(1/4) = 0 + 1/2 + 1/2 = 1. On average we expect 1 head in 2 flips of a fair coin. Worked example Air-conditioning units ordered x 0 1 2 3 4 5 P(X = x) 0.05 0.15 0.27 0.33 0.13 0.07 Multiply each value by its probability: 0, 0.15, 0.54, 0.99, 0.52, 0.35. Add: E(X) = 2.55 units per week. R x <- 0:5; p <- c(0.05, 0.15, 0.27, 0.33, 0.13, 0.07); sum(x * p) Python x = range(6); p = [0.05, 0.15, 0.27, 0.33, 0.13, 0.07]; sum(a*b for a, b in zip(x, p))"},{"t":"The expected value of a function of X","u":"module-3.html#function","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"For any function h(X), weight the values h(x) by the same probabilities. For a linear function the expectation passes straight through. E[h(X)]= x Dh(x)\\,p(x) E(aX+b)=aE(X)+b Proof of the linear rule. E(aX+b)= (ax+b)p(x)=a x\\,p(x)+b p(x)=aE(X)+b(1) . Worked example Profit on laptops A shop buys 3 laptops at $500 each and sells them at $1000 each; the manufacturer buys back unsold laptops at $100 each. X = number sold has PMF p(0) = 0.4, p(1) = 0.1, p(2) = 0.2, p(3) = 0.3, and the profit is h(X) = 1000X + 100(3 − X) − 1500. Simplify: h(X) = 1000X + 300 − 100X − 1500 = 900X − 1200. E(X) = 0(0.4) + 1(0.1) + 2(0.2) + 3(0.3) = 1.4. E[h(X)] = 900(1.4) − 1200 = 1260 − 1200 = $60. On average the shop expects $60 profit per cycle of buying 3 laptops. Watch out. In general E[h(X)] ≠ h(E(X)). The shortcut E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b works because h is linear; for h(X)=X2 , compute x2p(x) ."},{"t":"Variance and standard deviation","u":"module-3.html#variance","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"The variance of X is the expected squared distance from the mean; the standard deviation is its square root, in the units of X. The shortcut identity is usually faster by hand. Var(X)= X2= x D(x- X)2p(x) Var(X)=E(X2)-[E(X)]2 X= X2 Var(aX+b)=a2 X2 aX+b=|a|\\, X For any function h(X), Var[h(X)]= (h(x)- h(X))2p(x) . Worked example Variance of AC units E(X2)=0(0.05)+1(0.15)+4(0.27)+9(0.33)+16(0.13)+25(0.07)=8.03 . Var(X)=8.03-2.552=8.03-6.5025=1.5275 . X= 1.5275=1.2359 units. Worked example Spread of the laptop profit E(X2)=0+0.1+4(0.2)+9(0.3)=3.6 , so Var(X)=3.6-1.42=1.64 . Profit h(X) = 900X − 1200: Var=9002(1.64)=1,328,400 and =900 1.64=\\$1152.56 . The −1200 shifts every profit equally and does not change the spread. Red-pencil reminder. Square the multiplier for the variance ( a2 ), not for the SD (|a|). The added constant b never affects spread."},{"t":"The binomial distribution","u":"module-3.html#binomial","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"Use the binomial model when (1) there is a fixed number n of trials, (2) each trial is a success (S) or a failure (F), (3) the trials are independent, and (4) P(S) = p is the same on every trial. Then X = number of successes in n trials, and we write X (n,p) . The binomial coefficient counts the ways to choose which x trials are successes; px is the probability of those successes and (1-p)n-x the probability of the remaining failures. b(x;n,p)= nxpx(1-p)n-x x=0,1, ,n nx= n!x!(n-x)! B(x;n,p)=P(X x)= y=0xb(y;n,p) E(X)=np Var(X)=np(1-p)=npq X= npq Why: X is the sum of n independent Bernoulli(p) variables, each with mean p and variance p(1 − p). Means add to np; variances add to np(1 − p). Worked example Faculty sampling A university has equal numbers of male and female faculty. Each month one faculty member is chosen at random for a survey (anyone may be chosen again). X = number of women chosen in 30 months. Independent monthly choices with P(female) = 0.5: X (30,0.5) . PMF: P(X=x)= 30x(0.5)30 , x = 0, 1, …, 30. E(X) = 30(0.5) = 15 and X2=30(0.5)(0.5)=7.5 . P(X < 11) = P(X ≤ 10) = x=010 30x(0.5)30 0.0494 . Worked example Fraudulent transactions 18% of a company’s online transactions are fraudulent. X = number of fraudulent transactions in a random sample of 50, so X (50,0.18) . Expected number: E(X) = 50(0.18) = 9. P(2 < X < 8) = P(3 ≤ X ≤ 7) = b(3) + b(4) + b(5) + b(6) + b(7). For example, b(3)= 503(0.18)3(0.82)47=0.0102 . The five terms are 0.0102, 0.0262, 0.0530, 0.0872, 0.1203; their sum is 0.2969. R sum(dbinom(3:7, 50, 0.18)); pbinom(7, 50, 0.18) - pbinom(2, 50, 0.18) Python from scipy.stats import binom; binom.cdf(7, 50, 0.18) - binom.cdf(2, 50, 0.18) Watch out. Translate strict inequalities: P(2 < X < 8) starts at 3 and stops at 7. And check the four conditions; sampling without replacement from a small population is not binomial."},{"t":"The hypergeometric distribution","u":"module-3.html#hypergeometric","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"Use the hypergeometric model when sampling without replacement : a population of N items has exactly M successes, and a sample of n is chosen so that every subset of size n is equally likely. X = number of successes in the sample, X (n,M,N) . h(x;n,M,N)= Mx N-Mn-x Nn (0,n-N+M) x (n,M) E(X)=n MN Var(X)= N-nN-1\\,n\\, MN (1- MN ) The finite population correction N-nN-1 reduces the variance because of sampling without replacement; it is close to 1 when n is much smaller than N. Worked example Tagged tigers 13 of a population of 39 Bengal tigers were fitted with GPS collars. Later, 5 tigers are captured at random. X = number of tagged tigers among them: N = 39, M = 13, n = 5. P(X=0)= 130 265/ 395=65780/575757=0.1142 . P(X=1)= 131 264/ 395=13(14950)/575757=0.3376 . P(X < 2) = 0.1142 + 0.3376 = 0.4518. E(X) = 5(13/39) = 1.6667 and Var(X)= 3438(5) 13 23=0.9942 . R phyper(1, m = 13, n = 26, k = 5) (R’s n is the number of failures, N − M) Python from scipy.stats import hypergeom; hypergeom.cdf(1, 39, 13, 5) (arguments N, M, n)"},{"t":"The negative binomial distribution","u":"module-3.html#negbin","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"Run independent Bernoulli trials with constant success probability p until a fixed number r of successes has been observed. X = number of failures before the rth success, X (r,p) . nb(x;r,p)= x+r-1r-1pr(1-p)x x=0,1,2, E(X)= r(1-p)p Var(X)= r(1-p)p2 Worked example An urn with replacement An urn has 17 black and 8 red balls; each ball is replaced after it is drawn. X = number of red balls before the 5th black ball. Success = black, p = 17/25, r = 5. E(X)= 5(8/25)17/25= 4017 2.35 red balls. Worked example Recruiting six players An NBA team wants 6 players; each player asked agrees with probability 0.6. Find the probability that exactly 9 players must be asked. Nine asked means 6 acceptances and 3 rejections, the last one an acceptance: X = 3 with r = 6, p = 0.6. P(X=3)= 85(0.6)6(0.4)3=56(0.046656)(0.064)=0.1672 . R dnbinom(3, size = 6, prob = 0.6) Python from scipy.stats import nbinom; nbinom.pmf(3, 6, 0.6) Watch out. In our notes X counts failures, not total trials. If the question asks for the number of trials, it is X + r."},{"t":"The Poisson distribution","u":"module-3.html#poisson","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"The Poisson distribution models counts of events in a fixed interval of time or space when events occur one at a time, independently, at a roughly constant average rate. We write X ( ) . p(x; )= e- xx! x=0,1,2, E(X)=Var(X)= Binomial to Poisson. If X (n,p) with n → ∞, p → 0, and np → μ, then X is approximately Poisson(μ) with =np . Rule of thumb: the approximation is reasonable when n > 50 and np < 5 . Worked example Defective chips Each of 200 chips is defective with probability 0.01, independently. Find P(X ≤ 2). Check: n = 200 > 50 and np = 2 < 5, so use Poisson with μ = 2. P(X 2)=e-2 (1+2+ 222! )=0.1353(5)=0.6767 . The exact binomial answer is also 0.6767 to four decimals: the approximation is excellent here. R ppois(2, 2); pbinom(2, 200, 0.01) Python from scipy.stats import poisson, binom; poisson.cdf(2, 2), binom.cdf(2, 200, 0.01)"},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-3.html#summary","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A discrete random variable is described by its PMF or CDF. Its mean and variance come from probability-weighted sums, and linear changes follow E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b and Var(aX + b) = a²Var(X). Choose the model from the setting: fixed n independent trials (binomial), sampling without replacement (hypergeometric), failures before the rth success (negative binomial), or counts in an interval (Poisson). E(X)= x\\,p(x) Var(X)=E(X2)- 2 Bin: np,\\ npq Poisson: ,\\ R and Python for engineers. R: dbinom / pbinom , dhyper / phyper , dnbinom / pnbinom , dpois / ppois . Python (SciPy): binom , hypergeom , nbinom , poisson , each with .pmf and .cdf . Check the parameter order in each; they differ from the notes for the hypergeometric."},{"t":"Continuous random variables and the PDF","u":"module-4.html#continuous","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A random variable X is continuous if its possible values form an interval (or a union of intervals) of the real line. Its support is the set of values where the density is positive: the values X can actually take. Air temperature might take any value in [80, 100] °F; study time any value in [0, 300] minutes. Check that a function is a valid PDF and find a missing constant. Find probabilities from a PDF (by integrating) or from a CDF (by subtracting). Find percentiles and the median from the CDF. Compute E(X), E[h(X)], Var(X), and the SD by integration. Use the uniform, normal (with z and the table), gamma, exponential, and chi-squared models. A probability density function (PDF) f(x) gives probabilities as areas under its curve. P(a X b)= ab f(x)\\,dx f(x) 0 for all x - f(x)\\,dx=1 For any single value a, P(X=a)=0 : probability comes from intervals, not points. So for a continuous X, P(a X b)=P(a<X<b) . Watch out. f(x) is a density, not a probability. It can be larger than 1 (a narrow, tall curve); only the area under it must equal 1."},{"t":"The CDF","u":"module-4.html#cdf","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"The cumulative distribution function F(x) is the area under the density to the left of x. Where F is differentiable, its derivative is the density. F(x)=P(X x)= - xf(y)\\,dy F'(x)=f(x) P(X>a)=1-F(a) P(a X b)=F(b)-F(a) 0 F(x) 1 Worked example A density on [0, 1] Let f(x)=3x2 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 and 0 otherwise. Valid PDF: f(x) ≥ 0, and 01 3x2\\,dx= [x3 ]01=1 . CDF: F(x)= 0x 3y2\\,dy=x3 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 (0 below, 1 above). P(0.5 X 0.8)=F(0.8)-F(0.5)=0.512-0.125=0.387 . Worked example Finding a constant Suppose f(x)=cx for 0 ≤ x ≤ 2. Find c. 02 cx\\,dx=c [ x22 ]02=2c . Set it equal to 1: c = 1/2."},{"t":"The uniform distribution","u":"module-4.html#uniform","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"X is uniform on [A, B], written X U(A,B) , if every value in the interval is equally likely: the density is flat. f(x;A,B)= 1B-A,\\ A x B F(x)= x-AB-A,\\ A x<B E(X)= A+B2 Var(X)= (B-A)212 F(x) = 0 for x < A and 1 for x ≥ B. The mean and variance follow from the integrals in Section 5. Worked example Pipeline breaks A pipeline is 100 miles long and every location is equally likely to break. X = distance (miles) from the origin to the break. X U(0,100) : f(x) = 1/100 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 100, and F(x) = x/100 on that interval. P(X = 55) = 0: a single point has no area. P(32 < X < 43) = F(43) − F(32) = 0.43 − 0.32 = 0.11. R punif(43, 0, 100) - punif(32, 0, 100) Python from scipy.stats import uniform; uniform.cdf(43, 0, 100) - uniform.cdf(32, 0, 100) (scipy uses loc = A, scale = B − A)"},{"t":"Percentiles and the median","u":"module-4.html#percentiles","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"For 0 < p < 1, the (100p)th percentile η(p) is the value with area p to its left. The median is the 50th percentile: half the area lies on each side. p=F( (p))= - (p)f(y)\\,dy F( )=0.5 Worked example Percentiles of f(x) = 3x² The CDF is F(x)=x3 . 80th percentile: 3=0.8 , so =0.81/3=0.9283 . Median: 3=0.5 , so =0.51/3=0.7937 . Watch out. Set the CDF equal to p and solve for x. Setting the density f(x) = p does not give a percentile."},{"t":"Expected value and variance","u":"module-4.html#moments","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"For a continuous random variable, sums become integrals. E(X)= X= - x\\,f(x)\\,dx E[h(X)]= - h(x)\\,f(x)\\,dx Var(X)= - (x- X)2f(x)\\,dx Var(X)=E(X2)-[E(X)]2 X= Var(X) Worked example Mean and SD of f(x) = 3x² E(X)= 01 x 3x2\\,dx= [ 3x44 ]01=0.75 . E(X2)= 01 x2 3x2\\,dx= [ 3x55 ]01=0.6 . Var(X)=0.6-0.752=0.0375 , so X= 0.0375=0.1936 . The median (0.7937) is above the mean (0.75): the density is skewed left. R f <- function(x) 3*x^2; m <- integrate(function(x) x*f(x), 0, 1)$value; integrate(function(x) x^2*f(x), 0, 1)$value - m^2 Python from scipy.integrate import quad; m = quad(lambda x: x*3*x**2, 0, 1)[0]; quad(lambda x: x**2*3*x**2, 0, 1)[0] - m**2"},{"t":"The normal distribution","u":"module-4.html#normal","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"X has a normal distribution with mean μ and variance σ², written X N( , 2) , if its density is the symmetric, single-peaked bell curve below. Its support is (−∞, ∞). Changing μ shifts the curve; changing σ² changes its spread. About 68%, 95%, and 99.7% of the area lies within 1, 2, and 3 standard deviations of the mean (the 68–95–99.7 rule). f(x)= 1 2 \\, \\! (- (x- )22 2 ) E(X)= Var(X)= 2 The normal density has no closed-form antiderivative, so probabilities are computed numerically for the standard normal Z N(0,1) , whose CDF is written Φ(z), and every other normal is reduced to Z by standardizing. z= x- Z= X- N(0,1) A z-score counts how many standard deviations x lies from μ; negative means below the mean. Our convention: round z to two decimals, then read the left area Φ(z) from the table. Worked example Three-toed sloth weights Weights are normal with μ = 4 kg and σ² = 0.36, so σ = 0.6. P(X > 5): z = (5 − 4)/0.6 = 1.6667 ≈ 1.67. The table gives Φ(1.67) = 0.9525, so P(X > 5) = 1 − 0.9525 = 0.0475. P(3.7 < X < 4.5): z₁ = (3.7 − 4)/0.6 = −0.50 and z₂ = (4.5 − 4)/0.6 = 0.8333 ≈ 0.83. Φ(0.83) − Φ(−0.50) = 0.7967 − 0.3085 = 0.4882. Software with unrounded z gives 0.0478 and 0.4891; the small differences come from rounding z. R 1 - pnorm(5, 4, 0.6); pnorm(4.5, 4, 0.6) - pnorm(3.7, 4, 0.6) Python from scipy.stats import norm; norm.sf(5, 4, 0.6), norm.cdf(4.5, 4, 0.6) - norm.cdf(3.7, 4, 0.6) Working backward. To find the value with a given left area p, find the z with Φ(z) closest to p in the body of the table, then unstandardize: x= +z . For example, the 90th percentile of sloth weight uses z = 1.28: x = 4 + 1.28(0.6) = 4.768 kg. Red-pencil reminder. The table gives left areas. For “greater than,” subtract from 1; for “between,” subtract two left areas. Use σ, not σ², in the z-score."},{"t":"The gamma function and the gamma distribution","u":"module-4.html#gamma","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"For α > 0 the gamma function is ( )= 0 x -1e-x\\,dx . ( )=( -1) ( -1) (n)=(n-1)! ( 12)= X has a gamma distribution with shape α and scale β, X G( , ) , if its density is the one below. With β = 1 it is the standard gamma. Its CDF is the lower incomplete gamma function: F(x; , )=F(x/ ; ) , where F(·; α) is the standard gamma CDF. f(x; , )= 1 ( )x -1e-x/ ,\\ x 0 E(X)= Var(X)= 2 Worked example Gamma values (5)=4!=24 . ( 52)= 32 12 ( 12)= 34 =1.3293 . If X G(2,3) , then E(X) = 2(3) = 6 and Var(X) = 2(3²) = 18, so σ = 4.2426."},{"t":"The exponential distribution and memorylessness","u":"module-4.html#exponential","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"The exponential distribution is the gamma with α = 1 and β = 1/λ. We write X ( ) with rate λ > 0. f(x)= e- x,\\ x 0 F(x)=1-e- x,\\ x 0 E(X)= 1 Var(X)= 1 2 Memoryless property. For any s, t ≥ 0, P(X>s+t X>s)=P(X>t) . The remaining waiting time does not depend on how long we have already waited: for a component’s lifetime, P(X > 10 | X > 5) = P(X > 5). Why: P(X>s+t X>s)= e- (s+t)e- s=e- t=P(X>t) . Worked example Machine failure The time (hours) until a machine fails is X (0.2) . P(X > 8) = e-0.2(8)=e-1.6=0.2019 . P(3 < X < 6) = F(6) − F(3) = e-0.6-e-1.2=0.5488-0.3012=0.2476 . The mean lifetime is 1/0.2 = 5 hours. R 1 - pexp(8, 0.2); pexp(6, 0.2) - pexp(3, 0.2) Python from scipy.stats import expon; expon.sf(8, scale=1/0.2), expon.cdf(6, scale=5) - expon.cdf(3, scale=5)"},{"t":"The chi-squared distribution","u":"module-4.html#chisq","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"The chi-squared distribution with ν degrees of freedom is the gamma with α = ν/2 and β = 2, written X 2( ) . It returns in Chapter 7 for intervals for σ². f(x)= 12 /2 ( /2)x /2-1e-x/2,\\ x 0 E(X)= Var(X)=2"},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-4.html#summary","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A continuous distribution is described by a density whose areas are probabilities. Integrate f for probabilities and moments, set F(x) = p for percentiles, and standardize normal values with z = (x − μ)/σ before using the table. P(a X b)=F(b)-F(a) E(X)= x f(x)\\,dx z= x- Exp: P(X>x)=e- x R and Python for engineers. R: punif , pnorm / qnorm , pexp , pgamma(x, shape, scale = β) , pchisq , and integrate() . Python (SciPy): uniform , norm , expon(scale = 1/λ) , gamma(a = α, scale = β) , chi2 , and scipy.integrate.quad ."},{"t":"Two discrete random variables","u":"module-5.html#joint-pmf","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"When two random variables X and Y are defined on the same sample space, their joint probability mass function gives the probability of each pair of values. Find probabilities, marginal distributions, and expectations from a joint PMF or joint PDF. Decide whether two random variables are independent. Compute covariance and correlation and use Var(X ± Y). Distinguish a parameter from a statistic and a population distribution from a sampling distribution. Use E(X̄) = μ, σ_X̄ = σ/√n, the Law of Large Numbers, and the Central Limit Theorem. p(x,y)=P(X=x,Y=y) p(x,y) 0 x y p(x,y)=1 P ((X,Y) A )= (x,y) Ap(x,y) A can be any set of pairs, such as \\(x,y):x>2,\\,y<4\\ or \\(x,y): (x,y)>1\\ . Worked example A joint PMF table X \\ Y 0 1 2 3 4 0 1/2 1/16 1/32 1/32 1/32 1 1/16 1/32 1/32 1/32 1/32 2 1/32 1/32 1/32 1/32 1/32 P(X > 1, Y > 2): only X = 2 with Y = 3 or 4: 1/32 + 1/32 = 1/16 = 0.0625. P(max(X, Y) < 1): both X and Y must be 0: p(0, 0) = 1/2."},{"t":"Marginal PMFs","u":"module-5.html#marginal-pmf","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"The marginal PMF of X adds the joint PMF across each row (over all y); the marginal PMF of Y adds down each column (over all x). pX(x)= y p(x,y) pY(y)= x p(x,y) Worked example Marginals of the table Row sums: pX(0)= 2132=0.65625 , pX(1)= 632=0.1875 , pX(2)= 532=0.15625 . Column sums: pY(0)= 1932=0.59375 , pY(1)= 432=0.125 , and pY(2)=pY(3)=pY(4)= 332=0.09375 . P(X = 1) = 0.1875 and P(Y ≤ 1) = 0.59375 + 0.125 = 0.71875."},{"t":"Two continuous random variables","u":"module-5.html#joint-pdf","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"For continuous X and Y the joint PDF f(x, y) gives probabilities as volumes under its surface. f(x,y) 0 - \\! - f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy=1 P (a X b,\\ c Y d ) = ab\\! cd f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx The marginal PDFs integrate out the other variable. fX(x)= - f(x,y)\\,dy fY(y)= - f(x,y)\\,dx Worked example A joint density Let f(x,y)= x(1+3y2)4 for 0 < x < 2, 0 < y < 1. Valid: f ≥ 0 and 14 02 x\\,dx 01(1+3y2)\\,dy= 14(2)(2)=1 . P(0<X<1,\\ 14<Y< 12)= 14 01x\\,dx 1/41/2(1+3y2)\\,dy= 14 12 2364=0.0449 . P(X>1,Y<1)= 14 12 x\\,dx 2= 14 32 2=0.75 . Marginals: fX(x)= x2 on (0, 2) and fY(y)= 1+3y22 on (0, 1). P(X>1)= 12 x2\\,dx=0.75 ; P(0<Y< 14)= 12 ( 14+ 164 )=0.1328 ; and P(X=2)+P(Y=1)=0 : single values have no probability. R f <- function(x, y) x*(1 + 3*y^2)/4; integrate(function(x) sapply(x, function(u) integrate(function(y) f(u, y), 0.25, 0.5)$value), 0, 1)$value Python from scipy.integrate import dblquad; dblquad(lambda y, x: x*(1 + 3*y**2)/4, 0, 1, 0.25, 0.5)[0]"},{"t":"Independence of two random variables","u":"module-5.html#independence","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"X and Y are independent when the joint distribution factors into the marginals everywhere; otherwise they are dependent. p(x,y)=pX(x)\\,pY(y)\\ for all (x,y) f(x,y)=fX(x)\\,fY(y)\\ for all (x,y) Worked example Checking independence For f(x,y)= x(1+3y2)4 : fX(x)fY(y)= x2 1+3y22=f(x,y) on the whole rectangle, so X and Y are independent . For the joint PMF table: p(0,0)=0.5 but pX(0)pY(0)=0.65625(0.59375)=0.3896 . One failing pair is enough: X and Y are dependent . Watch out. Independence must hold for every pair. One pair where the product matches proves nothing; one pair where it fails proves dependence."},{"t":"Expected values of h(X, Y)","u":"module-5.html#expectation","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"E[h(X,Y)]= x y h(x,y)\\,p(x,y) E[h(X,Y)]= h(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy Worked example Expectations from the table From the marginals: E(X) = 0.1875 + 2(0.15625) = 0.5 and E(Y) = 0.125 + 2(0.09375) + 3(0.09375) + 4(0.09375) = 0.96875. E(X + Y) = E(X) + E(Y) = 1.46875. E(X2+Y2)=0.8125+2.84375=3.65625 . E(2X3+18)=2E(X3)+18=2(1.4375)+18=20.875 . Worked example A density with a constant Let f(x,y)=x+cy2 for 0 < x < 1, 0 < y < 1. 01\\! 01(x+cy2)\\,dx\\,dy= 12+ c3=1 , so c= 32 . E(XY)= 01\\! 01 xy (x+ 32y2 )\\,dx\\,dy= 16+ 316= 1748=0.3542 . E(X2)= 14+ 16= 512 and E(Y2)= 16+ 310= 715 , so E(X2+Y2)=0.8833 ."},{"t":"Covariance and correlation","u":"module-5.html#covariance","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"The covariance measures how X and Y vary together; the correlation rescales it to lie between −1 and 1. Cov(X,Y)=E[(X- X)(Y- Y)] Cov(X,Y)=E(XY)- X Y Cov(aX,bY)=ab\\,Cov(X,Y) Var(X)=Cov(X,X) Var(X Y)=Var(X)+Var(Y) 2\\,Cov(X,Y) X,Y=Corr(X,Y)= Cov(X,Y) X Y -1 1 If Cov(X, Y) = 0, X and Y are uncorrelated . Corr(aX + b, cY + d) = Corr(X, Y) when a and c have the same sign. Independent ⇒ uncorrelated, but uncorrelated does not imply independent. = 1 exactly when Y = aX + b for some a ≠ 0. Worked example Covariance for f(x, y) = x + 1.5y² E(X)= 13+ 14= 712 and E(Y)= 14+ 38= 58 . Cov(X,Y)= 1748- 712 58=- 196=-0.0104 . X2= 512- ( 712 )2=0.0764 and Y2= 715- ( 58 )2=0.0760 . = -0.0104 0.0764(0.0760)=-0.137 : a weak negative linear relationship. Worked example Two coin flips X and Y count heads on the first and second of two independent fair flips. Each of the four pairs (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) has p(x, y) = 1/4. E(X) = E(Y) = 1/2 and E(XY) = P(X = 1, Y = 1) = 1/4. Cov(X, Y) = 1/4 − (1/2)(1/2) = 0, so ρ = 0, as independence requires. Red-pencil reminder. Var(X − Y) adds the variances: Var(X) + Var(Y) − 2Cov(X, Y). Variances never subtract."},{"t":"Parameters, statistics, and random samples","u":"module-5.html#statistics","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"A parameter is a numerical characteristic of the population: the mean income of all airplane pilots in the US, or the percentage of all Texas college students with triple-digit student debt. It is fixed but unknown. A statistic is computed from a random sample, such as the mean income of a random sample of 50 pilots. It is our best guess for the parameter, and because it varies from sample to sample it is a random variable. A random sample X1, ,Xn is independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.). The difference between a statistic and its parameter is random sampling error . The population distribution describes the variable across all individuals; the sampling distribution of a statistic describes its values across all possible samples of the same size. Example question Speed of airplanes A study estimates the average cruising speed of commercial airplanes from a random sample of n = 10 planes. How much would the sample mean vary from sample to sample, and what happens to that variability as n grows? The next two sections answer both questions."},{"t":"The sample mean and the Law of Large Numbers","u":"module-5.html#sample-mean","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"For a random sample from a population with mean μ and variance σ², the sample mean is centered at μ and its spread shrinks with √n. E( X)= X= Var( X)= 2n X= n Law of Large Numbers. As n increases, X gets closer to μ: X as n → ∞. The uncertainty in the sample mean decreases as n increases. Worked example Polar bears Weights of Arctic polar bears have mean 450 kg and variance 9 kg2 . A random sample of n = 10 bears is taken. E( X)=450 kg. = 9=3 kg, so X=3/ 10=0.9487 kg. Watch out. Use σ (not σ²) in σ/√n. And σ/√n describes the spread of sample means, not of individual values."},{"t":"The Central Limit Theorem","u":"module-5.html#clt","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"Let X1, ,Xn be a random sample from a population with finite mean μ and finite variance σ². Whatever the shape of the population, the sampling distribution of X approaches a normal distribution as n increases. X N\\! ( , 2n )\\ for large n z= x- / n Any population (finite μ and σ²): X is approximately normal when n is large. Course rule: use the CLT when n > 40. Normal population: X N( , 2/n) exactly, for every n. Worked example A CLT probability Suppose polar bear weights have mean 450 kg and standard deviation 21 kg (shape unknown), and n = 49 bears are sampled. Find P( X>455) . n = 49 > 40, so the CLT applies: X N(450,\\ 212/49) . X=21/ 49=3 kg. z=(455-450)/3=1.6667 1.67 ; P( X>455)=1-0.9525=0.0475 . R 1 - pnorm(455, 450, 21/sqrt(49)) Python from scipy.stats import norm; norm.sf(455, 450, 21/49**0.5) Simulating it. Toss a fair coin 50 times and record the proportion of heads, Yi=Xi/50 with Xi (50,0.5) . Repeat the experiment 10, 100, 500, then 1000 times: the histogram of the Yi fills in a bell shape centered at 0.5. Watch out. The CLT is about the sample mean, not individual values. With n ≤ 40 from a population of unknown shape, do not assume X is normal."},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-5.html#summary","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"Joint distributions give probabilities for pairs; marginals add (or integrate) out the other variable; independence means the joint factors into the marginals. Covariance and correlation measure linear association. A statistic is a random variable with a sampling distribution: X is centered at μ with SD σ/√n, and for n > 40 it is approximately normal. pX(x)= y p(x,y) Cov=E(XY)- X Y = Cov X Y X N( , 2/n) R and Python for engineers. Joint tables: R m <- matrix(...); rowSums(m); colSums(m) ; Python m.sum(axis=1) , m.sum(axis=0) . Double integrals: Python scipy.integrate.dblquad . Simulate the CLT: R replicate(1000, mean(rexp(50))) ; Python np.random.default_rng().exponential(size=(1000, 50)).mean(axis=1) ."},{"t":"Point estimators and point estimates","u":"module-6.html#estimators","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"Let θ be an unknown population parameter. A point estimate is a single number that serves as a reasonable guess for θ. It comes from choosing a suitable statistic and computing it from the sample. That statistic is the point estimator , written . Because is computed from a random sample it has sampling variability: = +estimation error . Identify the parameter, the estimator, and the estimate in a study. Decide whether an estimator is unbiased and find its bias. Compare unbiased estimators by their variances; recognize the MVUE. Compute the standard error of x̄ and of p̂. Find method of moments and maximum likelihood estimators, and use the invariance property. Worked example Chevy Spark fuel economy A study wants the center of the MPG distribution of the new Chevy Spark and records the MPG of a random sample of 100 cars. Parameter: θ = μ, the mean MPG of all new Chevy Sparks. Estimator: = X , the sample mean MPG of 100 randomly chosen cars (a random variable). Estimate: the observed value x from this particular sample, for example 31.2 MPG. Watch out. The estimator is the rule (a random variable, like X ); the estimate is the number it produces for one sample (like x=31.2 )."},{"t":"Several estimators for the same parameter","u":"module-6.html#four-estimates","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"Different statistics can estimate the same parameter. For a symmetric population, the mean μ is also the median, so several sensible estimators exist. Worked example Dielectric breakdown voltage (Devore, Example 6.2) Twenty observations on breakdown voltage for pieces of epoxy resin are assumed to come from a normal distribution with mean μ: 24.46, 25.61, 26.25, 26.42, 26.66, 27.15, 27.31, 27.54, 27.74, 27.94, 27.98, 28.04, 28.28, 28.49, 28.50, 28.87, 29.11, 29.13, 29.50, 30.88 (a) Sample mean: x=555.86/20=27.793 . (b) Sample median: x=(27.94+27.98)/2=27.960 . (c) Average of the two extremes: (24.46+30.88)/2=27.670 . (d) 10% trimmed mean (drop the smallest and largest 2 values, then average): (555.86-24.46-25.61-29.50-30.88)/16=27.838 . Which is “best”? All four aim at μ; the next sections give the criteria: unbiasedness and small variance. R x <- c(24.46, 25.61, 26.25, 26.42, 26.66, 27.15, 27.31, 27.54, 27.74, 27.94, 27.98, 28.04, 28.28, 28.49, 28.50, 28.87, 29.11, 29.13, 29.50, 30.88); c(mean(x), median(x), mean(range(x)), mean(x, trim = 0.1)) Python import numpy as np; from scipy.stats import trim_mean; x = np.array([24.46, 25.61, 26.25, 26.42, 26.66, 27.15, 27.31, 27.54, 27.74, 27.94, 27.98, 28.04, 28.28, 28.49, 28.50, 28.87, 29.11, 29.13, 29.50, 30.88]); x.mean(), np.median(x), (x.min() + x.max())/2, trim_mean(x, 0.1)"},{"t":"Unbiased estimators","u":"module-6.html#unbiased","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"An estimator is unbiased when its sampling distribution is centered at the parameter, for every possible value of θ. Otherwise it is biased. Unbiased: E( )= Bias( )=E( )- Picture taking many random samples of 100 Chevy Sparks and computing x each time. If the population mean is 30 MPG, a histogram of all those sample means is centered very close to 30: X is unbiased. If X (n,p) , the sample proportion p=X/n is unbiased for p. For a random sample with mean μ and finite variance σ², the sample mean = X is unbiased for μ, and the sample variance S2= 1n-1 (Xi- X)2 is unbiased for σ². If the population is continuous and symmetric about μ, the sample median is also unbiased for μ. Worked example Blue whales Decibel levels are recorded for 50 blue whales; X = number above 180 dB, X (50,p) . In one sample X = 33. Point estimate: p=33/50=0.66 . Repeating the survey many times gives estimates p1, p2, p3, ; their histogram is centered at the true p, because E( p)=E(X)/n=np/n=p . Worked example Dividing by n is biased Let 2= 1n (Xi- X)2= n-1nS2 . E( 2)= n-1n 2 , so Bias=- 2n : it underestimates σ² on average. The bias shrinks to 0 as n grows. This is one reason the sample variance divides by n − 1."},{"t":"Minimum variance unbiased estimators","u":"module-6.html#mvue","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"Two desirable properties for an estimator are unbiasedness , E( )= , and minimum variance : among all unbiased estimators of θ, it has the smallest variance, so it varies least from sample to sample. An estimator with both properties is the minimum variance unbiased estimator (MVUE). Normal sample: = X is the MVUE of Worked example Two unbiased estimators of μ From a random sample X1, ,Xn with variance σ², compare 1= X and 2= X1+Xn2 . Both are unbiased: E( 2)= + 2= . Var( 1)= 2n and Var( 2)= 2+ 24= 22 . For n > 2, X has the smaller variance, so it is the better choice: it uses all the data."},{"t":"The standard error","u":"module-6.html#standard-error","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"The standard error of an estimator is its standard deviation. It measures the precision of the estimator: how much it varies from sample to sample. = Var( ) X= n p= p(1-p)n When σ or p is unknown, replace it by its estimate to get the estimated standard error , such as s/ n or p(1- p)/n . Worked example Standard errors Breakdown voltage: s = 1.462 with n = 20, so the estimated SE of X is 1.462/ 20=0.327 . Blue whales: p=0.66 , n = 50, so the estimated SE is 0.66(0.34)/50=0.067 ."},{"t":"The method of moments","u":"module-6.html#moments","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"For a random sample X1, ,Xn , the kth population moment is E(Xk) and the kth sample moment is 1n Xik . The mean is the first moment, and Var(X)=E(X2)-[E(X)]2 ; if the mean is 0, the variance is just the second moment. With m unknown parameters, the method of moments (MoM) sets the first m sample moments equal to the corresponding population moments and solves for the parameters. 1n i=1n Xij=E(Xj), j=1, ,m Worked example Three MoM estimators Normal N(μ, σ²). E(X)= and E(X2)= 2+ 2 . So = X and 2= 1n Xi2- X2= 1n (Xi- X)2 . Exponential(λ). E(X)=1/ = X , so =1/ X . Gamma(α, β). E(X)= and Var(X)= 2 . Matching X and 2= 1n (Xi- X)2 : = 2/ X and = X2/ 2 . Watch out. MoM matches moments with the divisor n: 1n (Xi- X)2 , not S2 ."},{"t":"Maximum likelihood estimation","u":"module-6.html#mle","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"For observed values x1, ,xn from a PMF or PDF f(x; ) , the likelihood function is the joint PMF or PDF of the data, viewed as a function of the parameters. The maximum likelihood estimators (MLEs) are the parameter values that maximize it: the values that make the observed data most plausible under the model. L( )=f(x1, ,xn; )= i=1n f(xi; ) L( ) L( ) for all Recipe. Write L, take = L (products become sums), set '( )=0 , and solve. Worked example Exponential MLE L( )= e- xi= ne- xi . ( )=n - xi and '( )= n - xi=0 . = n xi= 1 x : the same as the MoM estimator here. Worked example Normal MLEs ( , 2)=- n2 (2 2)- 12 2 (xi- )2 . Setting the partial derivatives to 0 gives = x and 2= 1n (xi- x)2 . The MLE of σ² divides by n, so it is slightly biased; for large n the difference from S2 is tiny."},{"t":"Invariance and large-sample behavior of the MLE","u":"module-6.html#invariance","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"Invariance. If 1, , m are MLEs, the MLE of any function h( 1, , m) is h( 1, , m) . h( )=h( ) = 2= 1n (xi- x)2 Worked example Using invariance Normal sample: the MLE of σ is 1n (xi- x)2 . Exponential lifetimes with x=5 hours: =0.2 , so the MLE of P(X > 8) = e-8 is e-8(0.2)=0.2019 . Large-sample behavior. For large n the MLE is approximately unbiased ( E( ) ) and approximately efficient: its variance is about as small as possible among unbiased estimators. So for large n, the MLE is approximately the MVUE."},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-6.html#summary","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"An estimator is a statistic used to guess a parameter. Prefer estimators that are unbiased and have small variance, and report precision with the standard error. The method of moments matches sample and population moments; maximum likelihood maximizes L(θ), and its estimates transform by invariance. Bias=E( )- X= n p= p(1-p)n Exp: =1/ x R and Python for engineers. Maximize a log-likelihood numerically: R optimize(function(l) sum(dexp(x, l, log = TRUE)), c(0.001, 10), maximum = TRUE) or MASS::fitdistr(x, \"exponential\") ; Python scipy.stats.expon.fit(x, floc=0) (returns scale = 1/λ̂) or scipy.optimize.minimize_scalar on the negative log-likelihood."},{"t":"standard normal table","u":"tables.html#formulas","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The standard normal table The entries give left area P(Z<z) , rounded to four decimals. Round z to two decimals. Negative rows use a negative hundredths digit too: row −1.6, column .04 means −1.64. Look up z → left area Left area → z Value Find in table For z beyond ±3.49, the course treats left area as essentially 0 or 1. For inverse lookups, choose the closest four-decimal entry. Ties use the midpoint z, such as .9500 → 1.645."},{"t":"formula sheet","u":"tables.html","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The formula sheet The formulas follow our course conventions. Work each one by hand first. Center x= 1n i=1n xi x=middle ordered value Spread s2= Sxxn-1 Sxx= (xi- x)2= xi2- ( xi)2n s= s2 Quartiles and outliers IQR=Q3-Q1 outlier if x<Q1-1.5\\,IQR or x>Q3+1.5\\,IQR Shifting and scaling y=x+C:\\ y= x+C,\\ sy=sx y=Cx:\\ y=C x,\\ sy=Csx Probability rules P(A')=1-P(A) P(A B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A B) P(A)= N(A)N (equally likely) Conditioning and independence P(A B)= P(A B)P(B) P(A B)=P(A B)P(B) independent: P(A B)=P(A)P(B) Total probability and Bayes P(B)= i P(B Ai)P(Ai) P(Aj B)= P(B Aj)P(Aj) i P(B Ai)P(Ai) Discrete random variables E(X)= x\\,p(x) Var(X)=E(X2)- 2 E(aX+b)=aE(X)+b Var(aX+b)=a2Var(X) Binomial b(x;n,p)= nxpx(1-p)n-x E(X)=np Var(X)=np(1-p) Hypergeometric h(x)= Mx N-Mn-x Nn E(X)=n MN Var(X)= N-nN-1\\,n MN (1- MN ) Negative binomial and Poisson nb(x)= x+r-1r-1pr(1-p)x E(X)= r(1-p)p Var(X)= r(1-p)p2 p(x; )= e- xx! E(X)=Var(X)= Continuous random variables P(a X b)= ab f(x)\\,dx=F(b)-F(a) E(X)= x f(x)\\,dx Var(X)=E(X2)- 2 F( (p))=p Uniform and normal U(A,B):\\ f= 1B-A E(X)= A+B2 Var(X)= (B-A)212 z= x- x= +z Gamma, exponential, chi-squared (n)=(n-1)! ( 12)= G( , ):\\ ,\\ 2 Exp( ):\\ F(x)=1-e- x 1 ,\\ 1 2 2( ):\\ ,\\ 2 Joint distributions pX(x)= y p(x,y) fX(x)= f(x,y)\\,dy indep.: p(x,y)=pX(x)pY(y) E[h(X,Y)]= h\\,p Covariance and correlation Cov(X,Y)=E(XY)- X Y = Cov(X,Y) X Y Var(X Y)= X2+ Y2 2Cov Sample mean and CLT E( X)= X= n X N\\! ( , 2n )\\ (n>40) z= x- / n Point estimation Bias( )=E( )- E( X)= E(S2)= 2 E( p)=p Standard error = Var( ) X= n p= p(1-p)n Moments and likelihood 1n Xij=E(Xj) L( )= f(xi; ) '( )=0 Exp: =1/ x Normal: 2= 1n (xi- x)2 Formulas for each chapter are added here as its module is completed. The t and χ² tables arrive with Modules 7 and 8."}];
